     5	      "observation": "Here's the result of running `cat -n` on /workspace/paper.md:\n     1\t# **Resource Usage and Optimization Opportunities** **in Workflows of GitHub Actions**\n     2\t\n     3\t\n     4\t\n     5\tIslem Bouzenia\n     6\t\n     7\tUniversity of Stuttgart\n     8\tStuttgart, Germany\n     9\tfi_bouzenia@esi.dz\n    10\t\n    11\t\n    12\t**ABSTRACT**\n    13\t\n    14\t\n    15\tContinuous integration and continuous delivery (CI/CD) has become a prevalent practice in software development. GitHub Actions is emerging as a popular platform for implementing CI/CD\n    16\tpipelines, called workflows, especially because the platform offers 2,000 minutes of computation for free to public repositories\n    17\teach month. To understand what these resources are used for and\n    18\t\n    19\twhether CI/CD could be more efficient, this paper presents the\n    20\tfirst comprehensive empirical study of resource usage and optimization opportunities of GitHub Action workflows. Our findings\n    21\tshow that CI/CD imposes significant costs, e.g., $504 per year for\n    22\tan average paid-tier repository. The majority of the used resources\n    23\tis consumed by testing and building (91.2%), which is triggered\n    24\tby pull requests (50.7%), pushes (30.9%), and regularly scheduled\n    25\tworkflows (15.5%). While existing optimizations, such as caching\n    26\t(adopted by 32.9% of paid-tier repositories), demonstrate a positive\n    27\timpact, they overall remain underutilized. This result underscores\n    28\tthe need for enhanced documentation and tools to guide developers toward more resource-efficient workflows. Moreover, we show\n    29\tthat relatively simple changes in the platform, such as deactivating\n    30\tscheduled workflows when repositories are inactive, could result\n    31\tin reductions of execution time between 1.1% and 31.6% over the\n    32\t\n    33\timpacted workflows. Overall, we envision our findings to help\n    34\timprove the resource efficiency of CI/CD pipelines.\n    35\t\n    36\t\n    37\t**CCS CONCEPTS**\n    38\t\n    39\t\n    40\t- **Software and its engineering** \u2192 _Development frameworks and_\n    41\t_environments_ ; _Software libraries and repositories_ .\n    42\t\n    43\t\n    44\t**ACM Reference Format:**\n    45\t\n    46\tIslem Bouzenia and Michael Pradel. 2024. Resource Usage and Optimization\n    47\tOpportunities in Workflows of GitHub Actions. In _2024 IEEE/ACM 46th_\n    48\t_International Conference on Software Engineering (ICSE \u201924), April 14\u201320,_\n    49\t_2024, Lisbon, Portugal._ [ACM, New York, NY, USA, 12 pages. https://doi.org/](https://doi.org/10.1145/3597503.3623303)\n    50\t\n    51\t[10.1145/3597503.3623303](https://doi.org/10.1145/3597503.3623303)\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\t**1** **INTRODUCTION**\n    55\t\n    56\t\n    57\tGitHub Actions is a powerful tool for developers to automate and\n    58\tstreamline their continuous integration and continuous delivery\n    59\t\n    60\t\n    61\tPermission to make digital or hard copies of all or part of this work for personal or\n    62\tclassroom use is granted without fee provided that copies are not made or distributed\n    63\tfor profit or commercial advantage and that copies bear this notice and the full citation\n    64\ton the first page. Copyrights for components of this work owned by others than the\n    65\tauthor(s) must be honored. Abstracting with credit is permitted. To copy otherwise, or\n    66\trepublish, to post on servers or to redistribute to lists, requires prior specific permission\n    67\tand/or a fee. Request permissions from permissions@acm.org.\n    68\t_ICSE \u201924, April 14\u201320, 2024, Lisbon, Portugal_\n    69\t\u00a9 2024 Copyright held by the owner/author(s). Publication rights licensed to ACM.\n    70\tACM ISBN 979-8-4007-0217-4/24/04...$15.00\n    71\t[https://doi.org/10.1145/3597503.3623303](https://doi.org/10.1145/3597503.3623303)\n    72\t\n    73\t\n    74\t\n    75\tMichael Pradel\n    76\t\n    77\tUniversity of Stuttgart\n    78\tStuttgart, Germany\n    79\tmichael@binaervarianz.de\n    80\t\n    81\t\n    82\t(CI/CD) processes. Since its release in 2019, it has been widely\n    83\tadopted by the community of developers, with more than 30% of\n    84\topen-source projects on GitHub utilizing it to automate their CI/CD\n    85\tworkflow [ 9, 23 ]. GitHub Actions enables developers to create workflows that are triggered by events, such as pushes, pull requests,\n    86\tand the opening of issues. Developers can customize workflows to\n    87\tperform a variety of tasks, e.g., building and testing code, deploying\n    88\tto various environments, and automatically creating releases.\n    89\tMost workflows execute on virtual machines (VMs) hosted by\n    90\tGitHub, many of which are offered for free to public projects. The\n    91\tcomputational resources offered to a free account include up to\n    92\t2,000 CPU minutes (33 hours) per month on virtual machines with\n    93\t7GB of main memory, 14GB of disk storage, and two or three CPU\n    94\tcores depending on the operating system. We refer to a repository\n    95\tas _free-tier_ if it uses only freely available resources, and as _paid-tier_\n    96\totherwise. Virtual machines also allow developers to test their code\n    97\tin a variety of environments and configurations, which improves\n    98\tthe dependability and stability of their software. Thanks to its\n    99\tintegration into the GitHub ecosystem and the offered computing\n   100\tresources, GitHub Actions has quickly emerged as a dominant\n   101\tCI/CD platform [15].\n   102\tThis popularity has motivated researchers to study the level of\n   103\tadoption of GitHub Actions, the repositories that use it, and the\n   104\ttasks executed therein [ 7, 9, 23, 39 ]. These studies are based on\n   105\tstatically analyzing workflow specifications, e.g., to quantify the\n   106\tworkflow scripts, jobs, and corresponding tasks. Other research\n   107\tfocuses on scrutinizing and characterizing the security of GitHub\n   108\tActions in comparison to other CI/CD platforms [4, 24].\n   109\tAn important aspect neglected by the existing literature are the\n   110\tcomputing resources consumed on the platform. In particular, it\n   111\tis currently unknown how many computing resources projects\n   112\ttypically consume, what these resources are used for, and what\n   113\tkinds of optimization opportunities exist. Obtaining insights into\n   114\tresource usage and optimization opportunities in workflows on\n   115\tGitHub Actions helps understand their impact on development\n   116\tcosts, and ultimately, contributes toward cheaper and faster CI/CD.\n   117\tThis paper presents the first empirical study that analyzes computational resource usage and optimization opportunities of workflows. Our study is based on a dataset of 952 repositories that\n   118\tperformed 1.3 million workflow runs over a period of 30 months,\n   119\twhich is the largest such dataset we are aware of. The study is\n   120\tdriven by three research questions:\n   121\t**RQ1: What amount of resources do workflows use and what**\n   122\t**are these resources used for?** The average annual cost of running\n   123\tworkflows for a paid-tier repository in our dataset is approximately\n   124\t$504, with the majority of executed jobs running on the least expensive Linux machines. Pull requests, pushes, and scheduled events\n   125\t\n   126\t\n   127\tICSE \u201924, April 14\u201320, 2024, Lisbon, Portugal Islem Bouzenia and Michael Pradel\n   128\t\n   129\t\n   130\t\n   131\tare the most common triggering events for workflows, accounting\n   132\tfor more than 90.0% of the total VM time. The most frequently\n   133\tperformed tasks are testing and building. While most workflows\n   134\tcomplete successfully, 10.0% to 17.4% fail, primarily due to errors\n   135\tin code during testing or building. These results show the considerable costs incurred by CI/CD platforms, which motivates studying\n   136\tpossible optimizations.\n   137\t**RQ2: To what extent do workflows use currently available**\n   138\t**optimization mechanisms, and what is their impact on the**\n   139\t**consumed resources?** We study and analyze both the prevalence\n   140\tand impact of six mechanisms that could help reduce the cost of\n   141\trunning workflows. The mechanisms range from sophisticated\n   142\toptimizations, such as caching, which is used by 32.9% of paid-tier\n   143\trepositories and reduces VM time by 3.4%, to simple configuration\n   144\toptions, such as setting a timeout value for a job, which is used by\n   145\t14.0% of paid-tier repositories, impacts 4.3% of runs, and reduces\n   146\tVM time by 8.1%.\n   147\t**RQ3: What further optimization opportunities exist and how**\n   148\t**much could they impact resource usage?** In addition to the existing optimization mechanisms, we investigate four techniques\n   149\tthat currently are not present on GitHub Actions. These optimizations could impact up to 4.5% of all runs and save up to 3.5% of\n   150\ttotal VM time, complementing the existing options studied in RQ2.\n   151\tThese findings show that there still are significant opportunities\n   152\tfor reducing both computational and monetary costs.\n   153\tThe results of this study could interest at least two groups of\n   154\tpeople. On the one hand, it will help developers in reducing the time\n   155\ttaken by their CI/CD pipelines, which decreases waiting time and\n   156\tspeeds up the development process. In addition, repositories that\n   157\trequire resources beyond those provided for free by GitHub have\n   158\tan interest in reducing the monetary costs imposed by workflows.\n   159\tOn the other hand, our results can guide platform providers, such\n   160\tas GitHub, when reducing their costs, especially the costs imposed\n   161\tby free-tier repositories.\n   162\tIn summary, this paper presents the following contributions:\n   163\t\n   164\t\n   165\t(1) The first study of the resource usage of workflows on GitHub\n   166\tActions, which differs from prior work that studies static\n   167\tproperties of workflows [7, 9, 23, 39].\n   168\t(2) A quantitative analysis of existing and potential optimizations of CI/CD pipelines that shows the (potential) prevalence and impact of ten different optimizations.\n   169\t(3) Actionable insights on how to reduce the computational\n   170\tresources consumed by workflows, which is relevant for\n   171\tboth developers and platform providers.\n   172\t(4) A reusable dataset of 1.3 million workflow runs from 952\n   173\trepositories, which can serve as a basis for further analysis\n   174\tand novel optimization techniques.\n   175\t\n   176\t\n   177\t**2** **BACKGROUND ON GITHUB ACTIONS**\n   178\t\n   179\t\n   180\tGitHub Actions is a platform for automating CI/CD (continuous integration/continuous deployment) tasks within the GitHub ecosystem. Developers use the platform, e.g., for automatically executing\n   181\ttests on every new commit or for regularly deploying applications.\n   182\tThe central concept of the platform are _workflows_, which are executable processes that perform one or more _jobs_ when a specific\n   183\t_trigger event_ occurs. The jobs within a workflow are either executed\n   184\t\n   185\t\n   186\t\n   187\tin parallel, sequentially as declared in the workflow, or in an order\n   188\tconstrained by dependencies between jobs. Each job consists of\n   189\tsequentially executed _steps_, each representing either a command,\n   190\tsuch as executing a bash script, or an _action_, i.e., reusable code that\n   191\timplements a common CI/CD-related tasks, e.g., checking out code.\n   192\tThe triggering events that start workflows include pushing to a\n   193\trepository, creating a new pull requests, and also scheduled events\n   194\tthat happen regularly, similar to a cron job.\n   195\tJobs are executed on _virtual machines (VMs)_, where each job is\n   196\texecuted inside its own VM runner. That is, two jobs cannot be\n   197\texecuted in parallel on the same VM. We call each execution of a\n   198\tjob a _job run_ or simply a _run_ . A workflow execution includes at\n   199\tleast one job run. GitHub offers VMs with different versions of\n   200\tLinux, macOS, and Windows, and also supports self-hosted VMs\n   201\ton a user\u2019s own infrastructure.\n   202\t\n   203\tAll computations performed on GitHub-hosted VMs are billed\n   204\ton a per-minute basis. For example, as of March 2023, running a\n   205\tminute of computation on a standard Linux VM (2 CPUs, 7GB RAM,\n   206\t14GB disk space) costs $0.008. In addition to CPU time, GitHub also\n   207\tcharges costs for disk space, [1] which we do not account for in this\n   208\tstudy. GitHub offers free resources to public repositories, e.g., 2,000\n   209\tfree minutes of computation to each unpaid user account. To help\n   210\tdevelopers manage the CI/CD costs, GitHub Actions offers several\n   211\toptimization mechanisms, e.g., caching, failing quickly if one out\n   212\tof a set of related jobs fails, or skipping workflows for particular\n   213\ttrigger events.\n   214\t\n   215\t\n   216\t**3** **METHODOLOGY**\n   217\t\n   218\t\n   219\tTo answer the research questions listed in Section 1, we gather a\n   220\tdataset of 1.3 million workflow runs from 952 open-source repositories on GitHub. To the best of our knowledge, this dataset is\n   221\tthe largest of its kind. The following describes our methodology\n   222\tfor gathering the dataset (Section 3.1), a set of metrics to quantify\n   223\tthe resource usage of workflows (Section 3.2), and a name-based\n   224\tanalysis to determine what CI/CD tasks a job performs (Section 3.3)\n   225\tfollowed by the methodology for estimating the prevalence and\n   226\timpact of optimizations (Section 3.4). Figure 1 gives an overview of\n   227\tour methodology.\n   228\t\n   229\t\n   230\t**3.1** **Data Collection**\n   231\t\n   232\t\n   233\tWe collect a dataset of workflow runs in three steps. First, we sample\n   234\t600 repositories from a set of 67K repositories collected by prior\n   235\twork [ 9 ], which have at least one workflow, 100 stars, and 100\n   236\tcommits. To also study less popular projects, we augment the initial\n   237\tset with 352 further repositories, which we obtain by randomly\n   238\tsampling 74K repositories with less than 100 stars from a larger\n   239\tset of 735K repositories [ 8 ] and by then keeping only those that\n   240\tare using workflows. The combined list results in a dataset of 952\n   241\trepositories. Second, we use the GitHub API to obtain all workflow\n   242\truns for each selected repository. The gathered information includes\n   243\tthe date and time of each workflow run, the workflow ID, the branch\n   244\tor commit that triggered the workflow, and other metadata. Third,\n   245\twe use the GitHub API to gather details about the jobs that were\n   246\texecuted within each workflow run. These data include, e.g., the\n   247\t\n   248\t\n   249\t1 [https://docs.github.com/en/billing/managing-billing-for-github-actions/about-](https://docs.github.com/en/billing/managing-billing-for-github-actions/about-billing-for-github-actions)\n   250\t[billing-for-github-actions](https://docs.github.com/en/billing/managing-billing-for-github-actions/about-billing-for-github-actions)\n   251\t\n   252\t\n   253\tResource Usage and Optimization Opportunities in Workflows of GitHub Actions ICSE \u201924, April 14\u201320, 2024, Lisbon, Portugal\n   254\t\n   255\t\n   256\t\n   257\tData sources /\n   258\t\n   259\tInputs\n   260\t\n   261\t\n   262\tMethodolgy\n   263\t\n   264\t\n   265\tRQs\n   <response clipped><NOTE>To save on context only part of this file has been shown t